<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:shell="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Shell;assembly=PresentationFramework">

    <!-- How far the drawing sits from the window's bottom-right corner. These windows are opaque and their
         window rectangle IS the visible card, so the lines belong a couple of DIP inside the real corner;
         the inset used to clear an 8 DIP transparent halo that no longer exists. A window whose corner is
         further in overrides this in its own resources (see QuickLookWindow).
         A DynamicResource rather than a StaticResource, so that per-window override wins: a template's own
         dictionary would resolve a static lookup to this value before the window ever got a say. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="ResizeGripInset">2,2,4,4</Thickness>

    <!-- The resize grip WPF draws for ResizeMode="CanResizeWithGrip", restyled.

         It is not a control of ours: Window creates a System.Windows.Controls.ResizeGrip (a Control, not
         a Thumb) and sizes it against the bottom-right corner whenever ResizeMode is CanResizeWithGrip,
         and that type takes its look from a theme style keyed by its own type. An implicit style here
         therefore replaces the theme's without any of that machinery being touched - and it has to keep
         the type, because the grip is what these windows resize by (see the direction setter below), so a
         window that dropped it would stop resizing altogether.

         Three stepped lines rather than the theme's dotted triangle: the theme's grip is a small field of
         dots that reads as decoration at this size, and the corner rule Windows itself draws is
         unmistakable and gives the eye something to aim at. Drawn faint, the same as the window chrome it
         sits against.

         ALIGNMENT IS THE POINT. The element Window creates spans the window's bottom-right corner, window
         rect included, so what a template puts inside it decides where the lines actually land. The style
         aligns the grip itself bottom-right and the Path inside it is inset by ResizeGripInset, which lands
         the lines just inside the visible card rather than in the transparent margin outside it. That margin
         is where a resize band would need WindowChrome to work, and it does not for a transparent window,
         so a grip drawn out there is one the pointer cannot reach.

         KEYLESS, AND IT HAS TO LIVE HERE rather than in a window's own dictionary. Resources/Styles.xaml
         is merged once by App.xaml, so this style is in Application.Resources and every window that asks
         Windows for a grip picks it up. That is what the grip needs, because seven windows set
         ResizeMode="CanResizeWithGrip" (search, quick panel, quick look, settings, both LocalSend windows,
         the plugin field prompt) and the grip is not a control any of them declares: a window can only
         inherit it, never restyle it by declaring something, so a per-window dictionary reaches just the
         windows that remember to merge that dictionary. -->
    <Style TargetType="{x:Type ResizeGrip}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Bottom"/>
        <!-- Declaring the direction is what makes this grip a handle at all. WindowChrome answers WM_NCHITTEST
             by asking the element under the pointer for this value BEFORE it looks at ResizeBorderThickness, and
             measured with a window parked off screen (WM_NCHITTEST at the grip's own centre) a ResizeGrip left
             at None answers HTCLIENT: the corner resized only because the 6px chrome border happened to cover the
             same corner. That border is also what swallowed the 6px overlay scrollbar pressed against the right
             edge of every one of these windows, which is why they now set it to 0 and this is the only thing
             left that resizes them. -->
        <Setter Property="shell:WindowChrome.ResizeGripDirection" Value="BottomRight"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="{x:Type ResizeGrip}">
                    <!-- The transparent Grid makes the whole grip box the target instead of just the three
                         stroked diagonals. The Path still stretches as the root's child exactly the way it did
                         as the root, so the drawing itself does not move. -->
                    <Grid Background="Transparent">
                        <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource WindowChromeText}" StrokeThickness="1" Opacity="0.5"
                              Margin="{DynamicResource ResizeGripInset}"
                              Data="M 12,0 L 0,12 M 12,4 L 4,12 M 12,8 L 8,12"/>
                    </Grid>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
